# {{ project_name }}

A Python project with a full quality gauntlet wired in, designed for
agent-driven development. Generated from
[python-templates](https://github.com/MarkGravestock/python-templates).

## Why

An agent will sometimes hallucinate an API, skip a test, lower the bar, or
declare work finished when it isn't. Reviewing harder doesn't scale;
deterministic checks do. Every gate in this project either passes or fails,
and prints output the agent can read and act on.

The same command, `uv run poe check`, is enforced at three points:

1. **While working.** AGENTS.md tells the agent work is not complete until
   the gauntlet passes, so it verifies and fixes its own findings before
   handing over.
2. **At commit.** The pre-commit hook runs the gauntlet, so unverified work
   cannot enter history even if the agent skipped step 1.
3. **In CI.** GitHub Actions re-runs it on Ubuntu and Windows.

Each gate catches a specific failure: ruff stops style drift, pyright stops
wrong types, import-linter stops the domain importing an adapter, radon fails
any function past grade C, and the coverage floor catches deleted tests.
Because the gates are deterministic, "done" means the same thing to the
agent, the hook, and CI.

## Quick start

```
uv run poe setup    # install dependencies
uv run poe check    # run the full gauntlet
uv run poe hooks    # install the pre-commit hook
```

## The gauntlet

| Task                    | What it does                                            |
| ----------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------- |
| `uv run poe check`      | Full gauntlet: lint → format → types → imports → complexity → tests |
| `uv run poe fix`        | Auto-fix lint and format issues                         |
| `uv run poe lint`       | ruff check, including flake8-bandit security rules      |
| `uv run poe format`     | ruff format --check                                     |
| `uv run poe typecheck`  | pyright (standard mode)                                 |
| `uv run poe imports`    | import-linter architectural boundaries                  |
| `uv run poe complexity` | radon cyclomatic complexity, fails on D/E/F grades      |
| `uv run poe test`       | pytest, quick, no coverage                              |
| `uv run poe test-ci`    | pytest + coverage floor + XML reports into `build/`     |
| `uv run poe audit`      | pip-audit: dependencies vs known-vulnerability databases |

## Staying current

- **Python**: track the latest stable release. `requires-python`, ruff's
  `target-version`, and pyright's `pythonVersion` move together; update all
  three at once.
- **Dependencies**: install the
  [Renovate GitHub App](https://github.com/apps/renovate) on this repository
  to activate `renovate.json`. Each update PR must pass the gauntlet before
  merge.
- **Vulnerabilities**: `uv run poe audit` checks the installed environment
  with pip-audit. CI runs it on every push and weekly on a schedule. It is
  deliberately not part of `poe check`: a new CVE in a dependency should
  fail CI, not block an unrelated commit.

## Mix-ins

Layer more structure from
[python-templates](https://github.com/MarkGravestock/python-templates) onto
this project later, e.g. the factory_boy/faker testing setup:

```
uvx copier copy --data template=testing-factories gh:MarkGravestock/python-templates .
uv add --dev factory-boy faker
uv run poe check
```

Available mix-ins: `testing-factories` (factory_boy/faker),
`testing-property` (Hypothesis), `testing-mutation` (mutmut, weekly CI),
`testing-containers` (testcontainers). Copier prints each one's remaining
steps after copying.

## Layout

The sample code (`Greeter` + a thin CLI) exists so every gate has real code
to check; replace it as your project grows.

```
uv run python -m {{ package_name }}.cli.main [name]
```
